From bef112997ec9caa40ce132175a780743b7147845 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 18:44:36 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] headerv2: add the BLAKE2b 164-byte header type and its
 hash

Bitcoin Knots PR #359 introduces a second block-header format. It is 164
bytes rather than 80, and its block hash is a staged BLAKE2b computation
rather than SHA256d over the header bytes. Both are consensus, so on a
chain that has activated it every header electrs stores, serves and
chains onto is one this codebase currently cannot parse or identify. It
has been live on testnet4 since height 149537.

This commit adds the type and the hash only. Nothing calls it yet; the
substitution for `bitcoin::block::Header` across chain/index/status/
electrum is a separate change, and keeping the two apart means the hash
can be reviewed against the published vectors without also reviewing an
80-file diff.

Why a local type. `rust-bitcoin`'s `block::Header` is a fixed six-field
struct with no room for the eleven added fields, and `bitcoin_slices`'
parser hardcodes 80 bytes; neither can be extended from outside the
crate. So `HeaderV2` lives here, depending on `bitcoin` only for the hash
newtypes and holding no electrs state, so it can be lifted out unchanged
if the format settles and upstream wants it.

`AnyHeader` is the union. It exposes exactly the accessors electrs reads
off a header today, so the substitution that follows is mechanical.

The format is self-describing: bit 31 of the version field, in the first
four bytes, marks v2. `header_size` reads it, and `AnyHeader::parse_all`
walks a concatenated run of mixed versions on that basis. This matters
beyond parsing convenience: it is why neither the server nor the client
needs to know an activation height, and why `blockchain.block.headers`
can keep returning a run of headers across the transition.

Testing, two independent oracles:

  - The five vectors published in Knots' `block_header_v2.json`, checked
    stage by stage rather than on the final hash alone, because the
    vectors carry every intermediate and a byte-order error then reports
    where it happened instead of just that it did. One did, on
    `m_xor_key`, during development. These cover all four ASIC profiles,
    both time-offset settings, and null and non-null XOR keys.

  - Four headers taken off live testnet4, frozen in as constants. The
    published vectors are generated by the same C++ this mirrors, so
    alone they prove the translation faithful but not the reading of the
    format correct; a live block hash is what the network agreed on.
    149537 is the activation block, 149538 chains onto it (so a wrong
    hash cannot also satisfy the next header's `prev_blockhash`), and
    169000 carries a non-zero `m_time_offset` with the time-rolling flag
    clear, which catches applying the offset unconditionally. The
    committed `m_height` field is a second check on the field offsets.

Every live testnet4 header so far is ASIC profile 0, solo mined, and
does not roll time. The test asserts that rather than leaving it
implicit, so if a miner starts using another profile it fails and says
to go find a live header for it.

One new dependency, `blake2b_simd`, which pulls in only
`constant_time_eq`. The digest length is part of BLAKE2b's parameter
block, so the 32-byte variant Knots uses is not a truncation of the
64-byte one.
